    },
    {
      "paperId": "958264bcee5aa5ef5f86f569b7c3ba4f489920b7",
      "title": "AI-vs-AI Cyber Defense: A Reinforcement Learning-Based Approach for Defending IoT Networks Against GAN-Generated Attacks",
      "abstract": "The rapid growth of the Internet of Things (IoT) has introduced critical security vulnerabilities, creating a landscape where static defenses are increasingly insufficient against sophisticated, automated threats. Traditional Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS) rely heavily on fixed signatures and pre-defined rules, making them largely ineffective against adaptive, AI-driven malware. Specifically, Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) can now produce novel, polymorphic attack vectors that bypass rigid security measures, leaving real-world networks exposed to zero-day exploits. To address this asymmetry, this paper presents a closed-loop \"Cyber Range\" environment that pits an autonomous AI defender against an evolving AI adversary. We utilize a GAN trained on the TON IoT dataset to act as the Red Team, continuously synthesizing novel attack traffic. Simultaneously, a Deep Q-Network (DQN) Reinforcement Learning agent (Blue Team) observes these attacks in real-time to learn dynamic mitigation strategies. Experimental outcomes confirm the viability of autonomous cyber defense; over 100 training episodes, the agent successfully converged on an optimal policy, transitioning from random exploration to confident decision-making. Our RL agent demonstrated a 92% success rate in adaptive threat mitigation and a 35% reduction in operational latency and energy costs compared to static security policies. These findings suggest that reinforcement learning can effectively navigate the complex trade-off between maximizing network security and minimizing resource consumption in constrained IoT environments.",
      "authors": [
