    {
      "paperId": "da5b36776bad4ab28a154b9d63ae6a1257278f82",
      "title": "An Adversarial Testing Framework for Multi-Agent Red–Blue Systems in Automated Software Hardening",
      "abstract": "Abstract - Software testing has advanced significantly, yet most automated methods remain limited to rule-based coverage rather than adversarial resilience. This paper introduces a Multi-Agent Adversarial Testing Framework (ATF) that simulates a Red–Blue team dynamic to continuously discover and patch vulnerabilities in software systems. Red Team agents generate targeted attacks and edge cases using adversarial reasoning and fuzzing. Blue Team agents respond with automated patch generation, refactoring, and test reinforcement. A Judge Agent evaluates both attack effectiveness and defense quality, forming a continuous self-improvement cycle. Our implementation integrates a large lan- guage model (LLM)-based reasoning with reinforcement learning and static analysis pipelines. Preliminary experiments on open- source projects show a 47% increase in unique bug discovery and a 33% reduction in recurring vulnerabilities compared with baseline fuzzers. These results suggest that adversarial multi-agent systems can significantly advance the robustness and adaptability of automated software testing.\n\nKeywords: Adversarial Testing, Multi-Agent Systems, Soft- ware Hardening, Reinforcement Learning, AI Agents, Continuous Integration.",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Muthukrishnan Thukkaram",
          "authorId": "2388211971"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2025,
      "venue": "INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF SCIENTIFIC RESEARCH IN ENGINEERING AND MANAGEMENT",
      "citationCount": 1,
      "referenceCount": 0,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/da5b36776bad4ab28a154b9d63ae6a1257278f82"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "958264bcee5aa5ef5f86f569b7c3ba4f489920b7",
      "title": "AI-vs-AI Cyber Defense: A Reinforcement Learning-Based Approach for Defending IoT Networks Against GAN-Generated Attacks",
      "abstract": "The rapid growth of the Internet of Things (IoT) has introduced critical security vulnerabilities, creating a landscape where static defenses are increasingly insufficient against sophisticated, automated threats. Traditional Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS) rely heavily on fixed signatures and pre-defined rules, making them largely ineffective against adaptive, AI-driven malware. Specifically, Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) can now produce novel, polymorphic attack vectors that bypass rigid security measures, leaving real-world networks exposed to zero-day exploits. To address this asymmetry, this paper presents a closed-loop \"Cyber Range\" environment that pits an autonomous AI defender against an evolving AI adversary. We utilize a GAN trained on the TON IoT dataset to act as the Red Team, continuously synthesizing novel attack traffic. Simultaneously, a Deep Q-Network (DQN) Reinforcement Learning agent (Blue Team) observes these attacks in real-time to learn dynamic mitigation strategies. Experimental outcomes confirm the viability of autonomous cyber defense; over 100 training episodes, the agent successfully converged on an optimal policy, transitioning from random exploration to confident decision-making. Our RL agent demonstrated a 92% success rate in adaptive threat mitigation and a 35% reduction in operational latency and energy costs compared to static security policies. These findings suggest that reinforcement learning can effectively navigate the complex trade-off between maximizing network security and minimizing resource consumption in constrained IoT environments.",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Awise Tahmid",
          "authorId": "2460358830"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "venue": "2026 International Conference on Power, Electronics, Communications, Computing, and Intelligent Infrastructure (PECCII)",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "referenceCount": 13,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/958264bcee5aa5ef5f86f569b7c3ba4f489920b7"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "74112860ddbde73113d90786c5b435f798edcf9e",
      "title": "Training Autonomous Cyber Defense Agents: Challenges & Opportunities in Military Networks",
      "abstract": "This paper addresses the development and training of robust autonomous cyber defense (ACD) agents within military networks. We propose an architecture that integrates a hybrid AI model comprising Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL), Large Language Models (LLMs), and a rule-based system into blue and red agent teams distributed across network devices. The primary goal is to automate key cybersecurity tasks such as monitoring, detection, and mitigation, thereby augmenting the capabilities of cybersecurity professionals in protecting critical military infrastructure. This architecture is designed to operate in modern network environments characterized by segmented clouds and software-defined controllers, which facilitate the deployment of ACD agents and other cybersecurity tools. The agent teams were evaluated in an Automated Cyber Operation (ACO) gym, which simulates NATO protected core networks and enables reproducible training and testing of autonomous agents. The paper concludes with an examination of the main challenges encountered in the training of ACD agents, with a particular focus on the security of the data and the robustness of the AI models during the training/testing phase.",
      "authors": [
        {
