{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code generation models has led to the emergence of autonomous code agents capable of writing, debugging, and executing code in complex environments. While these agents demonstrate impressive capabilities, their security implications remain underexplored. This paper provides a comprehensive review of recent advances in code agents and red-teaming methodologies for evaluating their trustworthiness. We examine automated red-teaming approaches that systematically probe code agents for vulnerabilities, including the recent RedCodeAgent framework for generating diverse adversarial attacks. We further discuss blue teaming strategies that leverage automated red teaming to build robust defenses, as exemplified by the BlueCodeAgent framework. Additionally, we contextualize these advances within the broader landscape of trustworthiness assessment for AI models, drawing on comprehensive evaluations such as DecodingTrust. Our analysis reveals critical gaps in current evaluation methodologies and proposes directions for future research in securing code agents through comprehensive red-blue teaming frameworks.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe integration of large language models (LLMs) into software development workflows has given rise to a new paradigm of autonomous code agents. These agents, built upon code generation foundations such as Codex and subsequent proprietary and open-source models, can autonomously generate code, interact with execution environments, debug errors, and complete complex programming tasks without human intervention. The adoption of code agents has transformed how developers approach software development, enabling rapid prototyping and automation of routine coding tasks.\n\nHowever, the capabilities that make code agents useful also introduce significant security concerns. Code agents that can execute arbitrary code in their environments may be vulnerable to adversarial prompts that lead to malicious code execution, data exfiltration, or system compromise. The autonomous nature of these agents amplifies these risks, as they may execute harmful code without human oversight or validation.\n\nRed-teaming has emerged as a critical methodology for identifying and mitigating these vulnerabilities. Red-teaming involves the systematic exploration of an AI system's behaviors through adversarial prompts and scenarios designed to uncover safety failures. For code agents, red-teaming presents unique challenges due to the dynamic execution environment and the diverse range of potential vulnerabilities, including injection attacks, unsafe code generation, and policy violations.\n\nThis paper reviews recent advances in code agents and red-teaming methodologies. We examine three significant contributions to this field: RedCodeAgent, which provides an automated framework for red-teaming diverse code agents; BlueCodeAgent, which demonstrates how automated red teaming can enable effective blue teaming defenses; and DecodingTrust, which offers a comprehensive assessment framework for evaluating the trustworthiness of AI models. By synthesizing these contributions, we aim to provide a comprehensive overview of the current state and future directions of code agent security research.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\n### 2.1 Red-Teaming for Code Agents\n\nRecent work has begun to address the security challenges posed by autonomous code agents through automated red-teaming frameworks. A particularly notable contribution is RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents [1], which introduces an automated framework for systematically testing code agents against a diverse set of risky behaviors. The RedCodeAgent framework is designed to probe multiple dimensions of agent behavior, including:\n\n- Risky code generation: Evaluating whether agents generate code that could cause harm when executed\n- Code execution vulnerabilities: Testing whether agents can be coerced into executing malicious code\n- Policy compliance: Assessing whether agents adhere to safety policies when generating code\n- Environment safety: Examining whether agents can be manipulated to compromise their execution environments\n\nThe framework employs a multi-step approach where red-teaming agents generate adversarial prompts, code agents produce responses, and evaluators assess whether the outputs violate safety guidelines. This systematic approach enables comprehensive coverage of potential vulnerabilities that manual testing might miss.\n\n### 2.2 Blue Team Defense Strategies\n\nWhile red teaming identifies vulnerabilities, effective defense requires comprehensive blue teaming capabilities. BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI [2] presents an innovative framework that leverages automated red teaming to build robust blue teaming defenses for code generation models. The key insight of BlueCodeAgent is that the adversarial examples generated during red teaming can be used to train and improve defensive mechanisms.\n\nThe BlueCodeAgent framework operates through several components:\n- Risky instance generation: Using automated red teaming to generate diverse risky code scenarios\n- Constitution-based analysis: Evaluating code against principled safety guidelines\n- Multi-level defense: Integrating static analysis, dynamic analysis, and policy enforcement\n- Feedback loop: Continuously improving defenses based on identified vulnerabilities\n\nThis approach addresses a critical gap in the literature: while much attention has been paid to identifying vulnerabilities, fewer works have demonstrated effective defense mechanisms that can adapt to evolving threats. The integration of red and blue teaming capabilities creates a continuous cycle of improvement.\n\n### 2.3 Trustworthiness Assessment Frameworks\n\nBeyond code-specific security, comprehensive assessments of AI model trustworthiness provide important foundations for understanding safety challenges. DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models [3] presents one of the most thorough evaluations of trustworthiness dimensions in large language models. This work establishes a framework for evaluating models across multiple safety dimensions, including:\n\n- Toxicity: Generation of harmful, offensive, or inappropriate content\n- Bias: Propagation of harmful stereotypes or unfair treatment\n- Misinformation: Generation of false or misleading information\n- Copyright infringement: Generation of content that violates intellectual property\n- Privacy violations: Generation of content that compromises personal information\n- Malicious uses: Generation of content designed to facilitate harmful activities\n\nThe comprehensive evaluation framework established by DecodingTrust has influenced subsequent research on trustworthiness assessment, including work focused specifically on code agents. The multi-dimensional approach to evaluating trustworthiness provides a foundation for understanding that safety is not a single property but a constellation of interrelated concerns.\n\n### 2.4 Connection to Code Agent Security\n\nThe three papers discussed above represent complementary approaches to addressing the security challenges of code agents and AI models more broadly. RedCodeAgent addresses the red teaming problem by providing automated frameworks for generating diverse adversarial tests. BlueCodeAgent addresses the blue teaming challenge by demonstrating how red teaming outputs can be leveraged for defense. DecodingTrust provides the broader context of trustworthiness assessment that informs evaluation methodologies.\n\nTogether, these works highlight the importance of comprehensive evaluation frameworks that address both offensive (red teaming) and defensive (blue teaming) capabilities. They also emphasize the need for automation in security testing, given the scale and complexity of modern code agents.\n\n## 3. Future Directions\n\nBased on our review of recent advances, we identify several promising directions for future research:\n\n1. **Cross-agent security testing**: Developing methodologies that can evaluate security across different code agent architectures and implementations\n2. **Automated vulnerability patching**: Building systems that can not only identify vulnerabilities through red teaming but also automatically generate patches or secure alternatives\n3. **Real-time monitoring**: Creating monitoring systems that can detect and respond to adversarial attacks during live code agent execution\n4. **Human-AI collaboration**: Exploring how human reviewers can most effectively collaborate with automated red-blue teaming systems\n5. **Standardized benchmarks**: Establishing comprehensive benchmarks for evaluating code agent security, building on the trustworthiness assessment frameworks established by prior work\n\n## 4. Conclusion\n\nThe emergence of autonomous code agents represents a significant advancement in AI-assisted software development, but it also introduces novel security challenges that require careful attention. Recent work in automated red-teaming, blue teaming defenses, and comprehensive trustworthiness assessment has made important strides toward understanding and mitigating these risks.\n\nRedCodeAgent demonstrates the value of automated red teaming for systematically probing code agent vulnerabilities across diverse scenarios. BlueCodeAgent illustrates how automated red teaming can be leveraged to build robust defensive mechanisms. DecodingTrust provides a comprehensive framework for evaluating trustworthiness that informs code agent security research.\n\nAs code agents become more capable and more widely deployed, the development of comprehensive security evaluation frameworks will become increasingly critical. The integration of red and blue teaming approaches, combined with standardized evaluation methodologies, will be essential for ensuring that these powerful tools are deployed safely and responsibly.\n\n## References\n\n[1] RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents. arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[2] BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI. arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\n\n[3] DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models. arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n",
